  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Functions \(R\) and \(D\) give the height, in feet, of a toy rocket and a drone, \(t\) seconds after they are released. Here are the graphs of \(R\) (for the rocket) and \(D\) (for the drone).</para>
<image alt="Two functions graphed on a cooridnate plane." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/6953fee5f8d02223583c335e6d1949b8e372de5f"/><list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Which of the inequalities is true: \(R(2)&gt;D(2)\) or \(D(2)&gt; R(2)\)?</item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> \(R(2)&gt;D(2)\)</para>
  <item>What was the height of the drone when the toy rocket hit the ground?</item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> 20 feet</para>
  <item>For what value of \(t\) is \(R(t)=D(t)\) true?</item>
  <para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> About \(t=4.5\).</para>
  <item>What does this tell you about the drone and the toy rocket?</item>
<para> <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis> At 4.5 seconds, the toy rocket and the drone are at the same height.</para>
</list>
</section>
